WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.155 --> 00:00:16.720
SARAH O'NEILL: When we talk about opioids, we refer to things like,

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:19.600
narcotics, is another name for opioids.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:26.090
And this includes medications like oxycodone or morphine, fentanyl.

00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:27.610
There are many opioids.

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:30.580
And all of them are used for pain.

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:37.990
And some of them are natural occurring opioids, such as morphine and codeine.

00:00:37.990 --> 00:00:41.830
And there are some that are semisynthetic,

00:00:41.830 --> 00:00:45.820
such as hydrocodone, oxycodone, and hydromorphone.

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:51.730
And then, we have fully synthetic drugs, like methadone and fentanyl.

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:57.640
So all of these drugs are used for both chronic cancer

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:03.640
pain and chronic noncancer pain, as well as in some acute situations

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:04.599
when it's appropriate.

00:01:04.599 --> 00:01:08.020
HOWARD J. SHAFFER: Opioid use is a very common medical treatment

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:11.710
for pain and a few other disorders.

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:18.730
If you need opioids, you should talk with your doctor about that need.

00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:21.590
And if it's specific to pain, for example,

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:23.680
you might ask whether there are other pain

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:28.060
relievers that are less powerful that will still do the job.

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:33.160
If you decide to undertake opioid treatment with your physician,

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:38.590
I think it's very important that you not exceed the prescribed dose

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:40.660
and the prescribed frequency.

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:45.760
Once you have taken opioids for a while, it's then important

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:48.430
not to stop without talking to your doctor

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:51.171
and developing a plan for reducing that use.

00:01:51.171 --> 00:01:53.421
SCOTT G. WEINER: Pain is one of the most common things

00:01:53.421 --> 00:01:55.210
that we treat in the emergency department.

00:01:55.210 --> 00:01:57.220
It's by far the most common reason why patients

00:01:57.220 --> 00:02:00.307
present to the emergency department.

00:02:00.307 --> 00:02:02.890
There's different studies, but definitely over 50% of patients

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:04.724
present because there's some sort of a pain.

00:02:04.724 --> 00:02:06.973
That could have been because they sprained their ankle

00:02:06.973 --> 00:02:09.580
and that hurts or their chest hurts or their abdomen hurts.

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:11.200
But it's pain.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:14.500
We as clinicians, want to do whatever we can to alleviate the pain.

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:18.190
This is part of our moral obligation.

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:22.420
We use medications like acetaminophen, ibuprofen as best we can.

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:26.010
But we do have this class of medications, which are the opioids.

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:30.700
Even in the course of my career, probably starting around 2000 or so,

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:34.210
there started to be this recognition that we were undertreating pain.

00:02:34.210 --> 00:02:37.090
Many studies came out that showed that we were allowing patients

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:39.500
to suffer needlessly.

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:41.764
We weren't giving them adequate medication.

00:02:41.764 --> 00:02:44.180
And this led to lots of different standards that happened.

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:48.370
There's an initiative that says, pain is the fifth vital sign, for example.

00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:51.640
They started asking about pain treatment on satisfaction surveys

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:52.765
that patients got.

00:02:52.765 --> 00:02:56.810
They started using it as a quality measure as well too.

00:02:56.810 --> 00:02:59.710
So I that what happened was clinicians, their first inclination

00:02:59.710 --> 00:03:02.680
was to use the medications that we knew worked for pain.

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:04.280
And those are opioids.

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:07.636
They're the most potent medications we have for pain.

00:03:07.636 --> 00:03:10.570
And so we started prescribing more and more.

00:03:10.570 --> 00:03:13.150
And I can tell you that we used to go from one or two

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:17.480
Tylenol with codeine to then using one or two tablets of oxycodone

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:21.310
to then using lots of hydromorphone, a really strong opioid.

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:24.460
And we started using more and more at a time,

00:03:24.460 --> 00:03:26.560
to the point where the pendulum definitely swung

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:29.470
too far to the side of overtreatment.

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:32.466
And now, we're in this phase right now.

00:03:32.466 --> 00:03:34.090
It's not just the emergency physicians.

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:37.160
We know that it's basically all clinicians.

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:39.400
We know that the prescribing of opioids increased

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:42.561
markedly, probably at least fourfold.

00:03:42.561 --> 00:03:44.560
If you look at the actual numbers of milligrams,

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:48.060
probably tenfold over the past 20 years.

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:52.030
And so now, we're in this area where we are right now.

00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:55.120
I think that the current strategy that we have,

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:58.810
at least in the emergency department, is to minimize the use of opioids

00:03:58.810 --> 00:04:00.190
as much as possible.

00:04:00.190 --> 00:04:04.480
So we do try and use acetaminophen, ibuprofen.

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:07.720
We use patches that have lidocaine that help with the symptoms.

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:11.860
We're trying to do a better job with regional blocks.

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:14.988
These are where we inject a little bit of anesthetic around a nerve.

00:04:14.988 --> 00:04:17.529
And this can make the paying for that area completely go away

00:04:17.529 --> 00:04:20.050
for a significant amount of time.

00:04:20.050 --> 00:04:24.270
We're also recognizing that we still need to use opioids in some situations,

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:26.110
but we don't need to use so much.

00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:29.250
We now know that acute pain, that really severe pain,

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:31.420
probably lasts only two to three days.

00:04:31.420 --> 00:04:35.200
And so there's no point in giving a prescription with 30 pills.

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:37.834
It's probably sufficient to have 8 or 10 pills.

00:04:37.834 --> 00:04:40.750
Because we know that those leftover 20 pills then stay in the medicine

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:43.780
cabinet, they get diverted, they get abused,

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:45.760
and that's where people get into trouble.

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:49.930
So I think that there's this new recognition about the problem.

00:04:49.930 --> 00:04:52.090
And I think there's a new sophistication with how

00:04:52.090 --> 00:04:53.950
we're using these medications as well.

00:04:53.950 --> 00:04:57.190
In addition to multimodal pain treatment, that's

00:04:57.190 --> 00:05:02.377
using every opportunity we have to treat pain before we reach for the opioid.

00:05:02.377 --> 00:05:04.960
Frequently, in the emergency department when someone has pain,

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:06.610
they want to feel better.

00:05:06.610 --> 00:05:11.230
And sometimes they equate feeling better with the opioid medications.

00:05:11.230 --> 00:05:12.980
This new era of sophistication where we're

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:15.236
trying to be very good about how we're using opioids,

00:05:15.236 --> 00:05:17.360
we're trying to reduce their utilization as much as

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:19.850
possible, that's sometimes tricky.

00:05:19.850 --> 00:05:22.340
Patients want to have the strong medication.

00:05:22.340 --> 00:05:23.780
They want a certain amount.

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:26.450
But we know that there's harm associated with it.

00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:29.660
If a patient comes in and they are requesting an opioid,

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:32.720
I now sit down with the patient and have a discussion.

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:36.560
We have good research that came out that showed that if you have back pain

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:40.940
and you get an opioid, your outcomes are exactly the same at five days as

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:42.470
if you hadn't had the opioid.

00:05:42.470 --> 00:05:45.740
So what's the point of having the risk of that medication?

00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:48.680
We know that if you maximize the nonopioid medications, ibuprofen,

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:52.670
acetaminophen, pain patches, like lidocaine patches,

00:05:52.670 --> 00:05:56.570
for example, then often, people will feel entirely better.

00:05:56.570 --> 00:05:59.060
So why take the risk of the opioid?

00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:02.600
The number that sticks in my head that I often tell patients is 15%.

00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:05.230
And I've seen that over and over again in many studies,

00:06:05.230 --> 00:06:09.710
whether it be having minor surgery, getting

00:06:09.710 --> 00:06:13.560
a first prescription for an opioid naive patient in the emergency department.

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:17.630
It seems that about 15% of them will still be on opioids at a year,

00:06:17.630 --> 00:06:19.610
even if they were opioid naive before and that

00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:21.560
was their very first prescription.

00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:24.394
So I tell patients, about 15% of patients

00:06:24.394 --> 00:06:27.310
that I give that first prescription to will still be dependent on them

00:06:27.310 --> 00:06:28.340
at a year.

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:30.290
It's not worth the risk in most cases.

00:06:30.290 --> 00:06:33.700
So when treating pain, opioids are extremely powerful.

00:06:33.700 --> 00:06:35.540
And there's clearly a role for them.

00:06:35.540 --> 00:06:38.390
The problem that we have currently is that practitioners

00:06:38.390 --> 00:06:40.700
are literally all over the map.

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:44.360
Some people will not prescribe anything for any condition.

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:50.150
And some people will give 30, 60, 90 pills of oxycodone or hydrocodone.

00:06:50.150 --> 00:06:52.220
And so I think what's happening is that we're

00:06:52.220 --> 00:06:56.930
creating this severe problem of expectations

00:06:56.930 --> 00:06:59.570
for patients, because they don't know what

00:06:59.570 --> 00:07:01.940
they're going to get from which doctor.

00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:03.770
We need to do a better job with that.

00:07:03.770 --> 00:07:07.190
We're starting to see guidelines that are coming up, such as from the CDC,

00:07:07.190 --> 00:07:08.510
which made guidelines.

00:07:08.510 --> 00:07:11.420
We need to get on the same page, even after procedures.

00:07:11.420 --> 00:07:16.520
For example, after a normal childbirth or a cesarean section,

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:20.150
we found that obstetricians are extremely varied

00:07:20.150 --> 00:07:23.510
with the numbers of pills that they give for patients.

00:07:23.510 --> 00:07:26.900
We see that after orthopedic surgery as well, after general surgery,

00:07:26.900 --> 00:07:29.040
like appendicitis, for example.

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:32.270
Some people were give no opioids and some will give a lot of opioids.

00:07:32.270 --> 00:07:34.100
And this is creating a problem.

00:07:34.100 --> 00:07:36.516
So we need some form of standardization.

00:07:36.516 --> 00:07:39.140
Obviously, we need to recognize that every patient is different

00:07:39.140 --> 00:07:40.670
and their needs will be different.

00:07:40.670 --> 00:07:43.370
But we can't have this tenfold difference

00:07:43.370 --> 00:07:46.790
in the amount of pain medications that are given to people.

00:07:46.790 --> 00:07:52.780
The thing that I'm most concerned about is not the treatment of acute pain,

00:07:52.780 --> 00:07:55.580
it's those leftover pills that are in the medicine cabinet,

00:07:55.580 --> 00:07:59.210
because we know that the vast majority of patients, people

00:07:59.210 --> 00:08:03.520
that nonmedically use opioids, they get them from a friend or family for free.

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:05.990
And what that means is that there were medications that

00:08:05.990 --> 00:08:08.780
were prescribed that were left over.

00:08:08.780 --> 00:08:11.690
We need to do a better job with rightsizing those prescriptions.

00:08:11.690 --> 00:08:13.460
And I think we're starting to do that.

00:08:13.460 --> 00:08:15.410
At my own hospital, we actually just started

00:08:15.410 --> 00:08:18.380
speaking about getting all of the specialties that prescribe,

00:08:18.380 --> 00:08:21.272
talking about their most common, most frequent procedures,

00:08:21.272 --> 00:08:23.480
and then getting all of the faculty on the same page,

00:08:23.480 --> 00:08:27.835
as far as what the guidance will be for how many pills should be prescribed.

00:08:27.835 --> 00:08:30.710
And the good thing about this is that they often emphasize nonopioids

00:08:30.710 --> 00:08:34.220
first and the opioids only for the severe pain, if the other medications

00:08:34.220 --> 00:08:36.080
and other modalities are not working.

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:38.809
Despite all the attention given to not prescribing opioids

00:08:38.808 --> 00:08:45.455
and to reduce our utilization of opioids, they clearly have a role.

00:08:45.455 --> 00:08:47.330
In the environment where I practice, we often

00:08:47.330 --> 00:08:52.970
see patients with severe chest pain, abdominal pain, bad infections,

00:08:52.970 --> 00:08:55.440
particularly trauma, fractures, for example.

00:08:55.440 --> 00:09:00.050
So it's clear from some of these problems that patients can have,

00:09:00.050 --> 00:09:07.130
fractures, severe trauma, abdominal pains, even cancer or chronic pain,

00:09:07.130 --> 00:09:09.760
there is certainly a role for opioids.

